# event_count.py: its own settings, ON TOP OF monitor.env.
#
# How the two files work together:
#   1. event_count.py first loads monitor.env, with monitor.py's own checks. From there it takes what both bots
#      share: TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN, TELEGRAM_CHAT_IDS, NAME, TIMEZONE and SHOW_TIMES. If monitor.env is missing a line,
#      the event count stops too (and says so on Telegram).
#   2. Then it adds this file, event_count.env: the ten settings below, which only the event count uses.
#   So a new bot token or a new chat id is changed once, in monitor.env, and both bots follow. Don't repeat
#   monitor.env's settings here; put only these ten.
#
# Copy to event_count.env next to event_count.py and monitor.py, fill in every line, then: chmod 600 event_count.env
# The real event_count.env is never in git. Every line is required: a missing or empty one stops the event count,
# and it says so on Telegram. One KEY=value per line; comments only on their own lines.

# --- when

# Required. When the count goes out, in monitor.env's TIMEZONE: times and ranges with a step in minutes,
# comma-separated, like monitor.env's STATUS_AT. Example: 17:00-19:00/15 (every 15 minutes during a 2-hour event)
EVENT_COUNT_AT=

# --- the registrations (one SELECT, read only)

# Required. The Docker container of the database with the registrations table. Example: app-db-1
EVENT_DB_CONTAINER=
# Required. Its user and its database, for psql (the same as you'd use to read it by hand). Example: postgres, postgres
EVENT_DB_USER=
EVENT_DB_NAME=
# Required. The event's id in the registrations table (its event_id column). Example: 2026-11-open-day
EVENT_ID=
# Required. A PostgreSQL regular expression matched against each email: matching rows are my own tests, left out of
# every count. Example: \+(og|test)   (any address with +og... or +test...)
EVENT_TEST_RE=

# --- the visitors on the page (one SELECT on Umami's own database, read only; no Umami token needed)

# Required. Umami's database container, its user and its database. Find them with:
#   docker exec <container> env | grep -E '^POSTGRES_(USER|DB)='
# Example: umami-db-1, umami, umami
UMAMI_DB_CONTAINER=
UMAMI_DB_USER=
UMAMI_DB_NAME=
# Required. The event page's website id in Umami (Umami -> Websites -> the site -> Edit; the same id is in the page's
# config.js, so it isn't a secret). Only this website's visitors are counted: staging's never are.
UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=
